Date: Sat, 04 Dec 1993 20:42:04 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: New York VR Expo '93

I attended the New York Virtual Reality Expo '93 at the New York Hilton
in Manhattan this past Thursday, and it was pretty disappointing.  About a 
third of the 50 or so exhibitions involved 1950's-style 3D glasses. Several
others required the user to look into a large cowl covering a normal PC screen,
with stereoptic images to convey a 3D appearance (or, as I thought of it,
a $5000 ViewMaster).  Worst of all was a _Lawnmower Man_ ripoff, in which the
user was harnessed to one of those huge gyroscope-thingies and given an
HMD to wear and a joystick to play with.  I was pretty excited to see this
demo until I noticed that the contraption's movements were being controlled not
by the user but by a bored-looking sales rep rocking the giant gizmo back and
forth.

The one interesting toy I got to play with was a package by Sense8 called
(I think) World Toolkit, which provides a set of C routines to allow you to
create your own virtual world.  I especially liked their HMD, which was much
smaller and less bulky than the Alien-like helmets favored by the other
vendors.  At $185,000, the prodcut isn't likely to be under most people's
tree this Christmas.  

Comparing this technology with the development of video games, I'd say things
are at the Pong stage right now.

-- Simon!

