From: c576653@monad.missouri.edu
Subject: META: Decline and Fall of the Extropians List?

tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May) writes:
>To put it bluntly, with that caveat in mind, I think the quality of
>debate on Extropians is seriously declining. I attribute this to the
>loss of key "idea" people like Tom Morrow, Hal Finney, Robin Hanson,
>and some others. And (apparently) remaining list members like Perry
>Metzger and Dean Tribble are being oftly quiet lately. (Though some
>idea people like Charlie Stross have returned, and I won't list the
>_ongoing_ idea people who remain, of which there are many.)
...
>I _really_ wonder what the 250 or so people who rarely post are
>getting out of the List. Will any of them comment?
...

I'm one of those 250 folks, and I sponge.  I like to read the  
discussions had by other list members and not contribute to the list  
much.  I've been here for a bit over two years and have contributed  
to a few conversations, but for the most part I stay silent.  I'm not  
interested in some of the topics, I'm not educated sufficiently for  
many of the "meaty" topics, and, while perhaps a bit thin-skinned, I  
don't appreciate the unnecessary neglect of manners used by some of  
the list participants.

I, too, have seen many names stop writing and regret their loss.  I  
feel that with (off the top of my head) the sole exception of Pandit  
Singh, they have been wrongly chased away whether actively or  
passively.  I think this is one of the main failings of this list.   
It can be attributed to the rudeness of list members, witch-hunts of  
various flavors, the justice system, or any of a number of other  
factors, but I see all of these as an over-simplification.  I think  
that it may be a common facet of our attitudes that, as evolved  
organisms, we tend to segregate, encouraging those not like us to  
depart.  This strikes me as something we may want to target for  
deletion on our road to transhumanism.  A sort-of genetic intolerance  
outside our awareness strikes me as a potentially undesirable trait.

I think that Perry's analogy between the list and a cocktail party  
has some merit, but isn't a necessity.  The lulls and peaks of  
interest have been happening since my tagging along began, and seem  
to happen on all the lists I read.  Some of them were strongly active  
when I started, but now only get three notes each week asking if the  
list is still alive.  As new members join, and new information is  
gathered on topics of interest, more conversations will keep  
starting.  Many of the topics of discussion are not strictly  
extropian, but are interesting to enough folks to keep the  
conversation going.  Many of the topics may be fluff, but again,  
enough interest keeps them going.  I look at the subject line of  
every message I get from extropians, I don't use any fancy ::exclude  
commands, I just read it if it sounds interesting.  This takes a  
couple hours every couple of days.  Well worth it.  What about paying  
$12/yr?  I'll still be here lurking.

