Subject: Iceland vs. West New Guinea (reply to David Friedman)

From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
>
>>Also, I'd like to explore the differences between
>>a true PPL and the Icelandic centralized quasi-PPL.  Which is better and
>>under what circumstances?  Is a centralized PPL better at promoting peace,
>>or does it contain the seeds for further dangerous centralization of power...
>(Mark Sulkowski)
>
>Iceland had a population of about 70,000. My guess is that that was too
>small to support a system of competing protection agencies and competing
>law. As Smith observed, the division of labor is limited by the extent of
>the market.

	You've read _The Enterprise of Law_, right?  On page 15, it is
mentioned that the Kapauku Papuans of West New Guinea had a population
of about 45,000.  I'll also quote from page 19:

	"Within the Kapauku, 'every functioning subgroup...has its own
legal system which is necessarily different in some respects from those
of the other subgroups. ...Because an individual...is simultaneously a
member of several subgroups of different inclusiveness (for example, a
Kapauku is a member of his household, sublineage, lineage, and political
confederacy...) he is subject to all the different legal systems of the
subgroups of which he is a member.'"

	Not only did the Kapauku have polycentric law, but population
did not seem to be a significant factor.  If they could have a PPL,
why not the Icelanders?

	It seems to me that there could be at least two possible reasons:

1) They simply chose to have a unified legal system.  Miller suggests
   that the various immigrants that settled Iceland in the early years may 
   have thought that the different legal traditions of their places
   of origin would only cause discord.  Or perhaps they simply liked
   the idea of unity.  Perhaps it gave them a cultural identity.

2) Perhaps the Icelanders had a lower population density than the Kapauku.
   If legal information could not spread as quickly through the population,
   then perhaps the Kapauku could handle polycentricism while the Icelanders
   could not.
 

