From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Subject: Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

<Ignore this line; it's fodder for the line-eater>

Kennita Watson said
  
 >  What role will physical affection play in the transhuman age?  (I
 >  don't just refer to sex here, though sex certainly plays a part -- I
 >  mean all the touches people exchange affectionately:  hugs, kisses,
 >  handshakes, pats on the back, consolatory strokes, running fingers
 >  through hair, etc.).  Will we find value in still having bodies that
 >  want touching?  If so, will we actually get together to do that
 >  touching, or will we rely on VR?

and a lively discussion on virtual fingers running through virtual hair
followed, but consider this. Perception happens in physical space (because
senses sense something physical) but also in perceptual space. There is no
reason why virtual perception has to happen through conventional senses. If
a sender and receiver agree to communicate through a new _sense_, created
just for the purpose of this communication with practice and a reliable
mapping to some conscious human I/O (and perhapa a lot of practice) no
reason why they can't.

For example imagine what I'll call finger/hair space.  I run my fingers
through your hair, you feel me; I feel you. (So far nothing meat can't do).
Next I send the perception of heat radiating my fingers. You respond with
heat-sinking coldness and shoot back the sensation of warm wetness. I
respond with a general broadcast of ambient misty wetness and turn up the
temperature. I throw in the sensation of being soaked in cosmic rays...etc.

OK so what's wrong with this picture? besides the weak way in which words
only partly convey my intent.

1. The parties have to agree on what the "senations" are in the new
perceptual space. And how to create them. In physical reality we have some
supposedly common set of experiences to draw from. In this reality we would
need new ways to create those standards.

2. Practice. We learn to move and dance and feel and see by trial and error
(ie feedback). Our senses such as seeing are intensely bidirectioal
(looking at something, creating mental models, evaluating mental models,
looking more closely, refining the mental models etc.) These new senses
need practice too.

3. Organization. Senses have to make sense. I don't mean this
tautologically. Vision provides a lot of information about the world in
relatively low bandwidth. Perhaps finger/hair space would not be a good
choice for a sense because it would take more time to master than it is 
worth and offers only limited extensions to physical sensations.

4. Bandwidth, especially outbound from the human sender. Here's the real
problem as I see it. We're just not that good at rapid transmission of
information. We can tak in a movie in 90 minutes or carry on an inner
fantasy as rich as a movie in the same amount of time. Sit down to write a
script however and we're talking months or years.

So what role should "sense" play in a world with a flexible definition of
what senses are; especially the senses we rely upon to communicate between
ourselves?

And what are the steps to make transhuman existance "human" in the best
warm, visceral, intimate, and respectful ways.


