Date: Sun, 05 Dec 93 00:29:43 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Love and Touch

on Kennita's excellent query: 
1) a theory of emotion backed by william james claimed that the emotion 
could be located in the body, rather than the mind. stated extremely, 
the claim was that, we dont cry bc we are sad, but are sad bc we cry. 
that is mistaken, but the general notion that bodily feedback amplifies 
and attracts an intensification of feeling seems true. i recall reading 
evidence that quadraplegics seemed to have a fairly constrained range of 
emotiveness, perhaps bc of the lack of intensifying cues from their 
body. 

2) i dimly recall a paper on the phenomenological differences between 
two sense modalities, vision vs. touch. perhaps this is an over-stated 
dualism, but the rift seemed apt: 
vision encourages an object-like reaction, distancing and distanced, 
clear awareness of self as separated from other
touch confuses boundaries, makes it difficult to sharply delineate self 
from other, lacks the localizing information of vision

electronic communication currently suffers from both the lack of bodily 
feedback and the ocular reactivity that prevents submerging interaction 
on the magnitude of real social spaces. for me, the key issue is from 
which conduits do we seek information?  by relying on your eyes, even in 
a 3D vr environment, you are likely to be more locked into the kinds of 
estrangement that typify visual modes of thinking. to fuse, and be 
exposed to the level of unpredictability and increasing exposure to less 
controlled interaction, create risky social environments, i.e., places 
where not every aspect can be consciously manipulated. this lines up 
with the matter of the body. we have multiple modes of perception, and 
each input transducer is ultimately piped into our verbal processing 
machinery. but just bc everything ends up there does not mean it should 
all be assumed to be happening there. the ultimate irony of the 
intellect is to become so powerful that it succeeds in sealing itself 
off from disconfirmatory feedback. 


