Date: Sat, 4 Dec 1993 23:13:23 -0800
From: moulton@netcom.com (Fred C. Moulton)
Subject: Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

The recent topic about love, Extropians and the physical interface covers
some interesting ground.  It is interesting to consider how physical
interaction, culture and emotional feelings relate.  Since the types of 
physical interaction and with whom it is practiced are defined differently
in different cultures, it is interesting to examine their relation as
cultures merge, change, die and are born.

All of this becomes even more interesting as we have a wider range of
experience of cultures and perhaps begin to change from the culture
in which we were raised.  This gets pushed even further when we 
consider changing one's race, sex and other parameters.  My question is
what are the philosopical foundations and tools one uses in approaching
these matters?  My initial reaction is that one could use something
analogous to Bartley's Pan Critical Rationalism to create and/or adapt
one's culture.

I consider one of the important aspects of a culture to be how it
handles other cultures.  Consider the physical act of a firm handshake
at a business meeting, is a sign of honesty or the sign of
boorishness?  My assumption is that if these physical interchanges are
meant to convey information, then as the cultural contexts become less
clearly defined perhaps the information conveyed will be as different.

And as individuals change their culture, does not the distinction between
culture and personality begin to fade?

Fred

P.S. I am sure that there are several readers poised to tell me that I
am over intellectualizing.  This is something that I am also considering.


