David Friedman inquired in msg #93-12-5,

    3. Speaking of bathing luxuries ...  . Another idea is a
    programmable thermostatic bathtub.  As anyone who soaks knows, a
    bathtub that is comfortable to get into is too cold once you get
    used to it and gets colder the longer you soak.  The usual kludge
    is to let out water, put in hot water, ...  . The obvious solution
    is a thermostatic heater.  You program it to start at the
    temperature comfortable to get in, gradually raise the temperature
    to what is comfortable for soaking, then maintain that.  I believe
    there are spas with thermostats, although I am not sure if any are
    programmable, but I have never heard of a home bathtub with one.

I have a product which provides something like this function at a low
price.  It's called a "Nirvana Personal Bath Massage."  It attaches
just ahead of the shower head. Through a switchable diverter, water
from the shower supply is directed through a flexible hose to a head
which attaches to the floor of the bathtub with suction cups. What
this device can do is raise the bath water temperature quickly and
uniformly, since it injects hot water mixed with air and tub water
into the tub in a high-speed stream that quickly mixes uniformly with
the tub water.  Then you can adjust it to keep the water as hot as
desired, while circulating it in the tub.  Since this arrangement adds
water to the tub, also supplied is a perforated flat rubber stopper
which is used to allow water to drain from the tub at a controlled
rate. (This is not a device for water conservation freaks).  If you
shut the unit off to allow tub water to cool naturally, replace the
perforated stopper with a conventional one.

Of course the temperature is regulated by adjusting your own hot/cold
water feed, not thermostatically. But I nevertheless find it much more
effective than just running more hot water from the tap, which
concentrates in one place. It's possible to raise temperature slowly
enough so that you can become used to a higher temp than you could
stand otherwise.  Certainly feels good after being outside in the
cold.

I got this device many years ago, so I'm not sure if it's still
available, or even if the manufacturer is still in business. But the
addresses given for the manufacturer in the Installation Guide that
came with it are:

