Another bathroom gadget that I enjoy is called a "Clen-Zone".  This
attaches through a diverter to the bathroom basin spout.  It directs
water through a narrow hose through another shutoff valve, to a nozzle
that attaches via sticky foam tape to the underside of the toilet
seat.  It delivers a thin stream of comfortably hot water where it
both feels good and minimizes the need for bathroom tissue.

I have a continuing relationship with this manufacturer, since some
spare part needs replacing on this unit once or twice a year, on
average. Fortunately, cost of spare parts is very reasonable. This
manufacturer is:

Hepp Industries
687 Kildare Crescent
Seaford NY 11783

(516)735-0032

In msg #93-12-44 Eli Brandt commented

    >From:  tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May) In any case, I'm pretty
    >skeptical of any nozzle-based local heater (let alone a cooler!).
    >The water is moving by too quickly for any system to locally alter
    >the temperature by more than tiny fractions of a degree.

    On-demand systems exist, though they aren't located in the nozzle
    itself.  I've heard they degrade ungracefully when you exceed
    their capacity, though.  Lesse, we're heating water maybe
    (115-60)5/9 ~= 30 degrees.  Each gallon/min is about 4 L/min =
    0.067 L/sec, so (67)(30)(4.184) ~= 8 kW per gallon/min.  This
    seems plausible.

I have some personal experience with these from visits to my wife's
family in Indonesia, where a central hot water supply is rare even for
relatively wealthy people.

My wife's parents in Medan (Sumatra) had a gas-fired "demand" heater
for the shower that did a pretty good job, although its thermostat was
rather sensitive.

On the other hand, my wife's brother in Jakarta had an electric unit
that frankly sucked. It required a five-minute warm-up and even so
failed to keep up for a decently long shower.

